{"annotationDigest":"sha256:b6cae7f5bf813016fa94e383e41e05b90f5ec31037f4cc33b3df66b0804a44b1","annotationId":"rann-921919420a5ebfa06fff894d05956d48","author":{"agent":"claude-code","kind":"ai","model":"claude-opus-5.5"},"createdAt":"2026-10-01T11:46:50Z","disclaimer":"AI-generated review opinion derived from the evidence package. It is not a verdict, does not change readiness, and must be confirmed by a human reviewer.","disclosureCheck":{"blocking":45,"contradictions":[],"disclosures":48,"nextDecision":"human_judgement","readiness":"blocked"},"findings":[{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"The only product hunk (b211d27b, byte-identical to the upstream #9056 diff at 0b4fe292 apart from the added `// Implements: SW-REQ-260925-XTGG` line) is `if (root.requestActive) root.finishRequest(null)` at the top of Menu.qml open(), before openDmenu()/openRoute() overwrite or clear selectionFile/doneFile/requestActive. finishRequest(null) captures the old done-file path, clears the request state and issues `: > <doneFile>` through resultProc, so the superseded bin/omarchy-menu-select caller leaves its poll loop. SW-REQ-260925-XTGG is new (added by our proof work, status review) and is the requirement that codifies this behaviour.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":33},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":132},{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"Upstream change: open() answers an in-flight select/input request as cancelled before dispatching the next summon (SW-REQ-260925-XTGG)","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG is in review.changes (added, matchLevel file).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"requirement":"SW-REQ-260922-50RE"},"type":"requirement_affected"},"detail":"Every changed requirement except XTGG is `modified` with an empty changedFields list and identical base/candidate description and FRETish clause (decisionSummary.intendedChange). Their YAML diffs are the evidence-backed trace re-review of the Menu.qml-linked requirements (commit 10d5ff90), made necessary because any Menu.qml edit stales every file-level link. The impact sidecar proof/impact-reviews/pr-9056.yaml records `no-authored-change` for the shared-ownership requirements. This is our proof work, not upstream code. Matched at file level only (Menu.qml is traced to all 29).","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-3JG5","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-3T3F","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-3VTN","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-4079","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-46HY","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-50RE","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-7NPE","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-8CQ4","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-B757","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-C8HX","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-DE93","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-DQ9P","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-E4J2","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-EFNR","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-FGZQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-JRW1","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-N3RM","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-NM45","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-SJ7P","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-TKDP","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-XW52","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-Z48F","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-Z680","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260928-8VJQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260929-B8N9","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260929-DXFJ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260929-T378","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260922-P708","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"proof/impact-reviews/pr-9056.yaml","kind":"file"}],"severity":"info","title":"The other 28 changed requirements carry trace re-review stamps only; their clauses are unchanged","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-50RE","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260922-50RE is in review.changes (modified, matchLevel file).","status":"confirmed"}},{"detail":"Fact statement (no claim type exists for it): review.diffCoverage is measured (mcdc:aggregate+bash+js+qml) but counts 0 changed decision lines, so the guard `if` at Menu.qml:33 is outside the coverage basis rather than covered. The two tests verifying XTGG (test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh and its requestHarness) have no observed outcome (disclosure test_outcome_unobserved:SW-REQ-260925-XTGG) because per-test results are not joined (test_results_not_joined). What the run does record: the new script is wired into the menu-shell suite (proof.yaml:161), and the run's MC/DC witness gates (mcdc_verifies_witnesses, mcdc_witness_drives_code) report passed with XTGG's rows claimed by that file. The script executes the real open/finishRequest/openDmenu/openExistingMenu/openRoute bodies under node vm with resultProc stubbed, so it does not exercise a real Quickshell Process.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":33},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","kind":"test"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"mcdc_verifies_witnesses","kind":"check"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Is the fix verified? The package holds no observed per-test outcome for the guard, and diff coverage counts no changed decision","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:open","shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:finishRequest"],"scenario":"three summons in quick succession on a live desktop: select request A answered by a pick, select request B opened while A's resultProc write is still running, then summon C arrives so open() calls finishRequest(null) for B while resultProc.running is still true"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"finishRequest() reuses the single shared resultProc: it sets resultProc.command and then running=true. If a previous answer write (e.g. request A answered by a pick, then request B opened, then summon C arrives within the write's lifetime) is still running, whether the new command starts is up to Quickshell Process semantics; if it does not, B's done file is never created and B's caller polls forever, the failure this PR is meant to remove. This is the busy-resultProc leg (SX26/C14/C15) that KI-MENU-REQUEST-LIFECYCLE keeps open; the PR does not address it. The vm witnesses stub resultProc with running always false, so they cannot settle it.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":148},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1022},{"id":"proof/known-issues/KI-MENU-REQUEST-LIFECYCLE.yaml","kind":"file"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: is the cancel write lost when resultProc is still running a previous answer?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Live re-summon while resultProc is busy\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:open","bin/omarchy-menu-select"],"scenario":"on a running Hyprland session, invoke omarchy-menu-keybindings 20 times in the background without answering, then count omarchy-menu-select processes after 2 s; expect at most one live picker"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"The PR body's two test-plan items (spam the Keybindings binding and confirm `ps aux | grep omarchy-menu-select` returns to 0; restart the shell and exercise open/select/cancel/reopen) are unchecked, and the upstream author notes ./test/all and the graphical acceptance suite were not run. No compositor-level evidence is in this package: the live harness test/shell.d/menu-compositor-test.sh is not in the menu-shell suite and its outcomes are unobserved here.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-select","kind":"file","line":91},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":33},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-compositor-test.sh","kind":"test"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: does the guard drain stuck pickers on a real desktop (the PR's own unchecked test plan)?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Key-repeat spam drains omarchy-menu-select processes\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-select","shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:finishRequest"],"scenario":"a pending omarchy-menu-select picker started by a bin/ caller (e.g. a theme or transcode picker) is superseded by a second summon; the caller receives exit 1 with empty stdout and must behave as on Escape"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"A superseded caller now sees its done file with an empty selection file, so bin/omarchy-menu-select exits 1 with no output (lines 95-99), exactly the Escape/cancel result (SW-REQ-260922-C8HX). Callers that treat exit 1 as 'user cancelled' get correct behaviour; a caller that retries, or that runs follow-up steps on failure, would act on a cancel the user never gave. 13 scripts under bin/ call omarchy-menu-select; none is changed by this PR.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-select","kind":"file","line":95},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-C8HX","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: do all omarchy-menu-select callers treat a superseded picker like Escape?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Superseded picker caller behaves as on Escape\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:open","shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml:openExistingMenu"],"scenario":"omarchy menu summon system (plain route payload) arrives while an omarchy-menu-select picker is open; the picker's caller exits 1 and the system menu opens"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"The guard fires on any summon, not only select/input: `omarchy menu summon system` while a picker is open answers the picker as cancelled and replaces it with the route menu. XTGG states this ('before it opens the new menu'), and the second witness block in menu-summon-cancel-test.sh asserts it, but whether this is the intended UX (vs. keeping the picker and ignoring the route summon) is a product decision for a human.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":37},{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","kind":"test"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: should a plain menu-route summon also cancel a pending picker?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Route summon over a pending picker (UX acceptance)\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"Reasoned from the code, not a package fact. resultProc.onExited closes the menu only when applySerial === requestSerial (Menu.qml:1025). open() calls the guard first and then openDmenu()/openExistingMenu(), both of which increment requestSerial (lines 911, 936), while the guard path does not touch applySerial. So the superseded request's write cannot close the new request's card. An action alias route (openRoute -> cancel -> runAction) is unaffected: by then requestActive is already false, so cancel() issues no second write.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":1025},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":911},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":936},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":973}],"severity":"info","title":"Opinion: the cancel write's onExited cannot close the newly opened menu","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"candidate":"unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","path":"test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","position":"intended"},"type":"unmapped_code"},"detail":"test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh is test code, not product behaviour. It carries `# Verifies: SW-REQ-260925-XTGG` in its header and on each witness block, with MCDC rows for XTGG and one `#mcdc:ignore:defensive` row reviewed in REVIEW-261001-SYQB. Its intent is the XTGG verification, so no new requirement or Implements trace is needed. A function-level trace on requestHarness would only refine it. This is our proof work, not upstream.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","kind":"file","line":5},{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Spec-gap candidate: the witness script is traced to XTGG by its Verifies markers (position: intended)","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","kind":"candidate"},{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh","kind":"file"}],"method":"review.scope (changed units, intent graph, spec-gap candidates): specGapCandidates","note":"The package flags unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh (unmapped_code, severity low): New file test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh has no function-level trace; only its file is traced (to 1 requirement(s): SW-REQ-260925-XTGG), which does not establish what this code is for. The author's position (intended) is judgement, not verified; a human decision settles it.","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"path":"test/node/report-reproducers.test.mjs","position":"spec_gap"},"type":"unmapped_code"},"detail":"Our proof work flipped the report #9057 expectation to exit 0 ('caller A was answered'), so this test now observes XTGG's behaviour end to end through the report script. The flip also removed its `// Reproduces: KI-MENU-REQUEST-LIFECYCLE` marker, leaving it untraced (part of disclosure unmapped_code). Add `// Verifies: SW-REQ-260925-XTGG` on that test so the observation counts as XTGG evidence.","refs":[{"id":"test/node/report-reproducers.test.mjs","kind":"file","line":62},{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Unmapped: test/node/report-reproducers.test.mjs now witnesses the fixed behaviour but traces to nothing (position: spec_gap)","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"test/node/report-reproducers.test.mjs","kind":"file"}],"method":"review.scope (changed units, intent graph, spec-gap candidates): units","note":"test/node/report-reproducers.test.mjs (modified) is traced to no requirement. The author's position (spec_gap) is judgement, not verified; a human decision settles it.","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"path":"proof.yaml","position":"noise"},"type":"unmapped_code"},"detail":"The proof.yaml change is one added line, `\"$B\" test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh;`, in project.commands.tests.menu-shell.command. It is test-suite configuration with no product behaviour, so a requirement trace would add nothing. The same edit is the source of the policy_changed_unclassified disclosure (next finding). Our proof work, not upstream.","refs":[{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":161}],"severity":"info","title":"Unmapped: proof.yaml registers the witness script in the menu-shell suite (position: noise)","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file"}],"method":"review.scope (changed units, intent graph, spec-gap candidates): units","note":"proof.yaml (modified) is traced to no requirement. The author's position (noise) is judgement, not verified; a human decision settles it.","status":"confirmed"}},{"detail":"The engine cannot classify the change to project.commands.tests.menu-shell.command, so disclosure policy_changed_unclassified prevents readiness, and the next decision is the human judgement \"Should the 1 protection reduction this change makes be accepted under the prior approved policy?\" by a policy_approver under approved_policy @ 67b0e44f6c15 (prior-policy signoff required). On its face the diff only appends one script invocation (proof.yaml:161), but that is the approver's call, not this review's. The edit is our proof work, not part of upstream #9056.","refs":[{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":161}],"severity":"high","title":"policy_changed_unclassified: the proof.yaml test-command edit needs a policy approver's judgement","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"checks":["obligation_decomposition_complete","obligation_evidence_complete"],"position":"likely_inherited"},"type":"check_attribution"},"detail":"obligation_decomposition_complete (42 obligations on 33 requirements covered by children, 5 deferred, 9 accepted KI-debt) and obligation_evidence_complete (58 covered, 6 deferred, 9 KI-debt) report the same counts on baseline run run-ace4a767 at 67b0e44f, and both disclosures are preExisting. They still block readiness as disclosed (check_failed:obligation_decomposition_complete, check_failed:obligation_evidence_complete).","refs":[{"id":"obligation_decomposition_complete","kind":"check"},{"id":"obligation_evidence_complete","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"The two failing obligation gates are inherited from the baseline (delta unchanged)","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"obligation_decomposition_complete","kind":"check"},{"id":"obligation_evidence_complete","kind":"check"}],"method":"gates (outcome, baseline delta, reason, failingTests) vs review.diff","note":"2 check(s): 2 confirmed, 0 refuted, 0 unverifiable. obligation_decomposition_complete also failed at the baseline run run-ace4a7675b750eb3ec28da0a284d5c1441f349bd2eb3deb976fb20d22115e17a: the failure pre-dates this PR. obligation_evidence_complete also failed at the baseline run run-ace4a7675b750eb3ec28da0a284d5c1441f349bd2eb3deb976fb20d22115e17a: the failure pre-dates this PR. Position: likely_inherited.","status":"confirmed"}},{"detail":"readinessReasons lists 11 missing_obligation entries (e.g. 4079/JRW1 external_call_timeout_bounded, Z680 atomicity, 8VJQ path_pair_agreement) without preExisting, and readinessBasis is 'introduced'. They remain blocking disclosures. However, the obligation gate counts are identical to the baseline (previous finding), the gaps are tracked deferrals (e.g. hazard-batch-D/47T8 for 4079), and none of the 11 requirements changed its clause. So they look like pre-existing debt that the package attributes to the change because Menu.qml is traced to these requirements at file level. Reported as an engine labelling question, not as a resolution.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-3T3F","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-4079","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-JRW1","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-N3RM","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-Z48F","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-Z680","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260928-8VJQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260929-B8N9","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260929-DXFJ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-260929-T378","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SYS-REQ-260922-P708","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"obligation_evidence_complete","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"The 11 missing_obligation blockers are the baseline's deferred obligations on requirements this change touches at file level","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"testResults.status is not_configured (project.checks.test_results.report_path is empty): 3 tagged tests, 0 joined. Claim evidence has 31 cells not_linked and 9 unobserved; XTGG's inherited error_handling obligation (from SYS-REQ-260922-X6Z5) is not_linked. Fixing this is the project's job: the test run must write the report at project.checks.test_results.report_path (with auto_link) and tag each verifying test with its obligation class (`<REQ>:<class>:<evidence>`). Until then no obligation here can be called covered.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260925-XTGG","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"tests_pass","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Per-test results are not joined, so none of the 40 in-scope obligation cells is observed","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"review.effects is absent (disclosure effects_unavailable), so no new_call_path / effect_gain / reachable_from claims are possible. The call-path statements above (open -> finishRequest -> resultProc; bin/omarchy-menu-select poll loop) come from reading the code, not from the engine. Scope units are file-granular for QML and shell (review.scope.approximations).","refs":[{"id":"review.effects.approximations","kind":"check"},{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":22}],"severity":"info","title":"Analysis bounds: change effects were not analyzed (QML/bash)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"Our proof work recorded that the guard fixes the re-summon leg (#9057: the report script now exits 0) while the busy-resultProc leg (C14/C15) and the doneFile-only leg stay open. Because the #9057 script no longer reproduces, the KI reproducer switched back to pocs/menu-qml-static.sh, a green tripwire that pins Menu.qml by static grep and does not execute either remaining leg. Opinion: the open legs therefore have no executing reproducer on this tree.","refs":[{"id":"proof/known-issues/KI-MENU-REQUEST-LIFECYCLE.yaml","kind":"file","line":20},{"id":"proof/evidence/ki-menu-request-lifecycle.yaml","kind":"file","line":9}],"severity":"info","title":"KI-MENU-REQUEST-LIFECYCLE stays open; its reproducer is now a static tripwire on this mirror","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:b09204d6a5c3def8a1948a411638233f67649e9a26eeee8043b58827b90a84d1","packageIdentity":"epkg-f6263576e56a5187520e254029f254db79cde1de6a7961a736d557a92481514d","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"The upstream product change is one guard reusing existing finishRequest plumbing. Its executing witnesses run the real function bodies, but no per-test outcome is joined and no compositor run is recorded. The busy-resultProc leg stays open. The package also carries a high-severity policy_changed_unclassified disclosure (a possible protection reduction from our proof.yaml edit) that a policy approver must judge, so readiness stays blocked."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":3,"rationale":"Settles whether the guard's cancel write is dropped when resultProc is still running (KI C14/C15 leg). Run on a real Quickshell instance with an artificially slow resultProc command.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":148}],"title":"Live re-summon while resultProc is busy"},{"forClaim":4,"rationale":"The PR's own unchecked test plan: confirms on a live compositor that every superseded caller exits.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-select","kind":"file","line":91}],"title":"Key-repeat spam drains omarchy-menu-select processes"},{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"Drive one representative bin/ caller of omarchy-menu-select, supersede it with a second summon, and assert it takes its cancel path (no retry, no follow-up action).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-select","kind":"file","line":95}],"title":"Superseded picker caller behaves as on Escape"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"Acceptance check with a maintainer: route summon cancels the picker and opens the route menu; confirm that is the wanted UX.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":37}],"title":"Route summon over a pending picker (UX acceptance)"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #9056 (FernandoJVideira, fixes #9057) adds one guard at the top of Menu.qml open(): an active select/input request is answered as cancelled (done file only) before the next summon replaces it. Without it, a repeated summon key leaves an omarchy-menu-select caller polling forever. The rest of this mirror is our proof work: SW-REQ-260925-XTGG plus executing vm witnesses (test/shell.d/menu-summon-cancel-test.sh, wired in proof.yaml), REVIEW-261001-SYQB, the impact sidecar, the #9057 reproducer flipped to exit 0, the KI note and static tripwire, and trace re-review stamps on 28 requirements.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** The package has no observed per-test outcome: test_results_not_joined, test_outcome_unobserved:SW-REQ-260925-XTGG, and diff coverage counts 0 changed decisions (the QML guard is outside the basis). MC/DC witness gates report passed for XTGG rows, but the witnesses stub resultProc and no compositor run is recorded.\n\n**Questions for a human.** Is the cancel write lost while resultProc is still busy (the open KI C14/C15 leg)? Does key-repeat spam drain pickers on a live desktop (the PR's unchecked test plan)? Do all bin/ callers treat a superseded exit 1 like Escape? Should a plain route summon cancel a pending picker?\n\n**Traceability.** 29 requirements changed (1 added, 28 with stamps only, all file-level). Open candidate: the witness script (intended, Verifies XTGG). Unmapped: report-reproducers.test.mjs (spec_gap: add Verifies XTGG) and proof.yaml (noise). Effects were not analyzed.\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** Readiness is blocked (introduced). The next decision is human_judgement (protection_weakened) by a policy_approver under approved_policy @ 67b0e44f6c15, on the unclassified proof.yaml test-command edit (our proof work). The 2 failing obligation gates are unchanged from baseline. The 11 missing_obligation blockers match the baseline's deferred counts but remain disclosed as blocking."}
